After all the discussion, I think I have a decent idea...

Let people invent their OWN races and classes.  You start with so many
"character points" (a la the RPG "GURPS") that you allocate to different
things, like your attributes, intrinsics, starting items, etc.  Basically,
it would be a shift from being AD&D-like to being GURPS-like.  Hmm...

Or, make race responsible for your intrinsics, and class responsible for
your starting items... so an elven wizard would start with, say,
infravision (sort of like telepathy but it works when you're not blind, and
has a range), and maybe stealth, from being an elf, plus a few magic
goodies for being a wizard.

SO there's two ideas.  Here's how the first one (points) could go...

All stats (attributes, like Strength, Constitution, and Wisdom) start at 8.
Raising them costs 5 points per point of increase.  Useful intrinsics (like
see invisible, magic-portection, and searching) cost, say, 20 points.
REALLY nice ones like teleport-control, polymorph and polymorph-control,
cost 40 or 50.  You start with about 80 points.  Instead of gaining
"experience points" to gain levels, you gain extra character points you can
up your attributes with instead.  HP could also be upped with character
points, as it is when you gain levels.


This would solve the "weak-wizard" problem - the idea that all users of
powerful magic are weaker than other types of characters.  It would let
people have more balanced characters, so they could have a better chance of
survival.

Another thing character points could buy:  LUCK!  Say, 25 points for a
luckpoint...Luck is valuable.


On the other idea:  race/class...

Races:
	Human (all stats start normal)
	Elf (+1 Int and Dex, -1 Str and Con)
	Dwarf (+1 Str and Con, -1 Int and Dex)
	Hobbit (+1 Dex and Con, -1 Str and Wis)
	Orc (+2 Str, -1 Int, -1 Wis)
	Ogre (+2 Str, +1 Con, -1 each of Int, Wis, Dex)
	Troll (-2 each of Int, Wis, Cha, but gets Regeneration)
	Half Elf (+1 Dex, -1 Con)
	Half Orc (+1 Str, -1 Wis)
	Faerie (-1 Str, Con, and Wis, +1 Cha and Dex, can levitate at will)
	Robot/Android (+2 Str, Dex, and Con, CANNOT USE SPELLS or SCROLLS!)

Intrinsics for Races:
	Elf:  Infravision (see above)
	Dwarf:  Weaker Infravision, plus Searching
	Hobbit:  strong Searching, +1 Luck to start with
	Orc:  hmm...Anyone?
	Ogre:  Again...any suggestions?
	Troll:  Regeneration
	Half-Elf:  Weak Infravision
	Half-Orc:  ???
	Faerie:  Toggle-able Levitation
	Robot/Android:  Weak Regeneration, Weak Searching, Weak Infravision

Some new Classes:
	Mechanic:  starts with a blessed wrench, a +1 plaid shirt, and a
container of grease (when used, creates a "grease slick" trap that makes
monsters slip and fall, slowing and injuring them and unwielding their
weapons) and a jack (lifts boulders).
	Traveling Salesman:  starts with a blessed briefcase of holding,
a random weapon of low quality but high saleable price (at a shop).
Intrinsically gets better prices for selling items at shops, but can only
sell five items at one shop before getting kicked out.
	Missionary:  Starts with a Holy Book of (deity name), which has (n)
charges and can be read like a fortune cookie; a wand of taming (5), a wand
of part waters (5), which dries up wet spots and kills water demons,
ability to turn undead (but it takes three rounds of #scream) and ability
to call down fire from Heaven (or up from Hell) upon any @, once.  (The
Wizard is, of course, immune.)
	Rockstar:  Starts with either a +1 cone bra (female) or a +1
leather jacket (male), a blessed cordless mike (repels @s like turn-undead
repels undeads, but never destroys), can use #scream (when in the Holy
Temple of Fender) to summon three tamed Backup Vocalists.

And then my new extended command, #scream:
	For most people, moves monsters one square away (as when a fountain
gives you bad breath) but can only be used once per 100 turns (or you lose
your voice and cannot cast spells or #scream for 100 turns), but
Missionaries can use it in three successive turns to "cast out demons" and
turn Undead.  (it does leave them vulnerabe those three turns, though...)
Rockstars can only use it in the temple of their god, Fender, to summon
Backup Vocalists.

Well, this is long enough now...

